    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right),\\ \\sqrt2\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi{12}\\right),\\ \\sqrt2\\sin\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right)$.\n\nEquivalently, $x=1,\\ \\frac{\\sqrt3-1}{2},\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt3+1}{2}$.\n\nThe substitution gives $\\sin3\\theta=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$; retain three distinct values of $x$.",
      "worked": "Let $x=\\sqrt2\\sin\\theta$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4\\sqrt2\\sin^3\\theta-3\\sqrt2\\sin\\theta+1&=0\\\\4\\sin^3\\theta-3\\sin\\theta&=-\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\\\\\sin3\\theta&=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\\\3\\theta&=\\frac\\pi4,\\ \\frac{3\\pi}4,\\ \\frac{9\\pi}4,\\ \\frac{11\\pi}4,\\ \\frac{17\\pi}4,\\ldots\\\\\\theta&=\\frac\\pi{12},\\ \\frac\\pi4,\\ \\frac{3\\pi}4,\\ \\frac{11\\pi}{12},\\ \\frac{17\\pi}{12},\\ldots\\end{align*}$$\nThe angles $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\frac{11\\pi}{12}$ repeat the sine values from $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{12}$. The next distinct value is $\\sin(\\frac{17\\pi}{12})=-\\sin(\\frac{5\\pi}{12})$.\n\nThus the three distinct roots are\n$$x=\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right),\\quad\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi{12}\\right),\\quad\\sqrt2\\sin\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right).$$\nUsing the difference and sum identities at $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, these are\n$$x=1,\\quad\\frac{\\sqrt3-1}{2},\\quad-\\frac{\\sqrt3+1}{2}.$$\nThree distinct roots account for all roots of the cubic.",
